WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:03.188
People have a hard time talking about money because

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.220
Money is the root of all of our problems

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:12.640
My relationship with the money is I don't like understand money ... (laughs)

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.480
Where did my other sock go?

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.840
My name is Quinn, I'm 22 years old and I live in San Francisco

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.060
I'm a software engineering intern at a small startup

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.020
Do you mind telling me how your day started?

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.380
I woke up and then... (laughs)

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.740
Went in my car to get something and found out that it'd been towed

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:51.480
(laughs)

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.019
And how much did that cost?

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.920
I got a 90 something dollar discount because it was the first time my car had been towed

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.080
and it still came out to almost 600 dollars

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.640
So we're starting the day at—

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:04.340
(sighs)... Yeah

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:05.240
Background guy: Minus $600?

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.180
(laughs) ... Minus 600

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.620
I'm paid $2,000 a week. So for a 40-hour week, that's $50 an hour

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:24.810
So computer science is something I had just dabbled in as a requirement for my math degree and then I just decided to do it

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.600
And he's with me as well

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.250
I'm Dolly Avenue. I am 23 years old and I live in Los Angeles, California

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:48.200
I make a living using my camera to take pictures

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.200
So the great thing about freelancing is you never really know how much you're gonna make

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.600
How much do you think you make a month?

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.740
Um... Four or five K

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.220
So I like C++, but I don't get to work in C++ a lot

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.200
What kind of programming do you want to go into?

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.360
Probably beta application

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.600
Oh gosh, good luck ... (laughs)

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.100
I'm missing my keys...

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.200
(laughs in relief?)

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.240
Could've been bad

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.980
Hi! You look cute. Oh my gosh, what is happening?

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.880
Getting into the office I probably spend about 30 minutes to an hour just figuring out what I was doing ...

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:34.500
(laughs)

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.500
last time I was working

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.720
I like where I work a lot right now

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:42.570
Do I love coding?

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.220
No, it's too much of how the sausage is made ... (laughs)

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.420
Thank you for the tea

00:02:53.160 --> 00:03:00.000
So today we were shooting with Brooke who wanted to make a calendar for her boyfriend

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.180
Do you have bubbles?
Brooke: Yeah

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:04.620
Nice

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:06.340
This is gonna be fun

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.440
Fiit 530 East 8th Street, I'm assuming this way

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.760
Such a good friend ... (laughs)

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:18.440
Sweet, thanks.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.080
Aww, it's an intimate space

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.360
No straight on

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:37.599
More angled, you and me sitting and stuff. Anyways, yeah be cool

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.800
Yeah, just let me know if anything else but let's get it

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:43.300
(laughs)

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.240
My favourite thing in the photograph hands down is people

00:03:51.799 --> 00:03:58.509
I absolutely love the energy and understanding somebody. I think that's what— what makes me keep creating

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.499
Takes so much for someone to be

00:04:09.859 --> 00:04:12.279
completely confident and vulnerable

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:20.529
So allowing me into their space and capturing their soul in a sense, I think that's rewarding

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.820
These look great and if you smile, smile that way okay

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.020
3, 2, 1 go. Smile.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:29.860
(background chatter)

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.880
It's falling on you ... (laughs)

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:35.820
(chatting about engineering)

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.880
Anybody else upgrade to X 26. Oh my

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.180
Friend: It's working swimmingly

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:41.060
(laughs)

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:47.160
Um.. can I grab a spinach and goat cheese quiche and a matcha latte please
Worker: That'll be $13.00

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:55.078
I think that people problems are more interesting than technical problems

00:04:55.089 --> 00:05:00.118
So my favorite part of my day as a software developer is when I got to solve the problem with someone else

00:05:01.209 --> 00:05:03.539
so I guess I could be happier if I was just

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.300
teaching high school

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.580
What's stopping you from pursuing that?

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:15.940
I think it's important that you aim for things you're passionate about, but that need not be grounded in your career

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:18.568
Like I would not want my parents to feel

00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:21.419
insecure about my future

00:05:22.299 --> 00:05:28.469
After my third semester of college, my dad was diagnosed with multiple myeloma, which is a type of bone marrow cancer

00:05:28.469 --> 00:05:32.578
And I'm his only child. So it fell on me to take care of him

00:05:33.129 --> 00:05:35.129
Luckily, he came out the other side

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:40.859
There is no way I could value two years in the middle of my life over two years potentially at the end of his

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:48.420
My decision to take care of him was very like logical because that's the type of person I am. That's the way I solve problems

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.140
That's okay. Wait, it's fizzing.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.440
(yawns)

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:16.920
Like we're the Kardashians and we're just having somebody. Oh man, ha, he can hear me.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.920
These are gonna look so good

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.880
I had a tough life because my parents were incompatible

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:40.180
Emotional abuse, physical abuse on top of that they didn't have an education

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:46.940
they're trying to find a job trying to find a place to live and having children to take care of

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:51.340
Our circumstances put us in situations that weren't ideal

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:57.020
That's how we grew up. And from there we just kind of learned how to survive

00:06:58.669 --> 00:07:02.379
I think like with money if you make the effort to change

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:10.149
your mentality and your lifestyle that goes even with your career and if you're open-minded to change like

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.720
You can pursue anything that you put your mind to

00:07:14.599 --> 00:07:16.599
That's where I'm trying to do with art

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.110
Look back at me

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.940
Perfect, gotta Photoshop your friend out

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:44.060
(claps)
Brooke: Thanks guys
Dolly Avenue: Woooo

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:46.400
Thanks for waiting

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:50.820
La la la laa

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.800
Do you believe that money defines yourself or or others?

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:00.980
Yes

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.960
Money is part of what turns you into who you are

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.620
Um, in almost every person that I've ever met

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:15.610
Like I wish it were different

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:26.050
I don't think money defines you, I think your character defines you, I think how you treat people defines you

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.710
And also how you treat yourself defines you

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:34.479
It's kind of ingrained in us, right

00:08:34.910 --> 00:08:41.829
money is quite literally how much you are worth ... (laughs) ... no matter how you try to spin it like there is a number

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.900
And I'm sure to some degree we all say that it doesn't— like money can't buy happiness

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:50.380
All right, but a lack of money can lose you some happiness ... (laughs)

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:52.700
Are you happy?

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:56.020
I think I'm happy. I wake up and

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.140
look forward to my day

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:06.820
Could I be happier? Yes, absolutely. Like it's not in my nature to pin a point. What would make me happier?

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:11.859
I think everybody wants to strive for more

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:16.809
I think I'm happy in terms of I'm making art and I'm getting paid for it.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:24.430
I didn't really have anything and I was happy so because of that at the end of a day, I don't really

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.720
feel like

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:30.940
hmm...what's the question?

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:35.740
So, what's your day looking like?

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.969
If you could do the math in your head?

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.100
$400 a day every day. How much  should I spend?

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:49.060
48 bucks plus the 600 from this morning puts us at 650 dollars -ish

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.800
So minus $250.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:56.180
How do you feel about that day? 
Quinn: You know besides the car it was pretty good ... (laughs)

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.360
(singing) No freedom caged bird

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:08.460
How much money did you make today on that shoot?

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:13.540
Well for the most part, the three hours I made four hundred and she paid for the

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.110
studio with just 80 dollars. We're still deciding

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.200
moving forward with the image cost

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:34.280
How is it?

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:37.240
It's really good

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:40.660
(singing) Heard you're scared of the downside

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:45.240
I know the darkness can keep you in

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.830
Sometimes the doubt of the blurred lines

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:52.360
can keep the

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:55.349
sunlight from creeping in

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.580
From creeping in

